#
# D-145 — THE GUARD'S MEMBERSHIP STOPPED AT THE RUN DIRECTORY, AND THE RUN'S
# SPEC DOES NOT LIVE THERE.
#
# `_artifact_guard` runs at the top of every orchestrator entry point precisely
# so no individual reader has to remember to check. Its membership was
# `fdir.rglob("*")` — derived over the run directory on both axes, and blind to
# everything outside it. `foundry_validate_castings` falls back to
# `Path(project_root) / state["spec_path"]` when the run dir holds no spec.md
# and then read it unguarded, so a file the run DECLARES and every door then
# reads was outside every guard in the module.
#
# This is not a hypothetical path: thunder-viper's own state.json carries
# spec_path "forge-specs/foundry-run-process-fixes/spec.md", outside
# foundry-archive/thunder-viper/. Driven through the real _DISPATCH at d8215c5,
# one non-UTF-8 byte in that file RAISED UnicodeDecodeError across the MCP
# boundary while `_run_artifact_problems(fdir)` returned [] with the broken
# file sitting right there. Every in-run-dir case was correctly refused by
# name; this was exactly the residue the rglob could not reach.
#
# TWO HALVES, AND WHY BOTH ARE NEEDED.
#
#   The READER is made total. `spec_path.read_text` and the second unguarded
#   read in the same module (`prompt_path.read_text`, covered today only
#   because a casting prompt happens to sit inside the run dir) now go through
#   `foundry_state.read_text_file` and return `document_refusal`. "A guard runs
#   first" is a fact about one call site, not a property of a reader — and
#   `_spec_requirement_ids`, which the DONE gate's requirement count runs
#   through, reads the same external file and never calls the guard at all.
#
#   The GUARD's membership is widened, so the answer is not "this reader
#   remembered" but "every door refuses by name". And the widening is DERIVED
#   OVER THE STATE DOCUMENT, not over the key: every string leaf of state.json
#   that resolves to an existing FILE is an input this run declared. Naming
#   `spec_path` would close this instance and leave the next declared input —
#   a context file, a research root, a migration source — outside the guard on
#   the day it is added, which is the escalated class exactly. The table's last
#   row plants a key nobody has ever heard of and the guard names it anyway.
#
# The project root is derived from `fdir` rather than passed in, and the
# ARCHIVE_DIR check is what makes that a fact about the path instead of an
# assumption about it. The control rows keep the refusal narrow: a run whose
# declared spec is healthy — which is every run this repo has — is untouched.
